        <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="325HW12">
            <title>Math 325 Homework 12 (Cauchy and Dedekind)</title>

            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Due December 1, 2026 (submission on Canvas).
                </p>
<p>
This assignment has the following readings:
<ul>
    <li>
        <p>
            Theorem III in Chapter 6 and Theorem I in Note III of Cauchy's <em>Cours d'Analyse</em>
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
           <em>Essays on the Theory of Numbers</em> by Richard Dedekind. 
        </p>
    </li>
</ul> 
</p>
                <p>
                    <term>Generative AI is forbidden on this assignment.  Struggling with it with only your classmates will help your understanding.</term>
                </p>
            </introduction>

<page>
<exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            In this exercise we want to analyze two arguments for Cauchy.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Carefully read the example that Cauchy gives for Theorem III (the Alternating Series Test) in Chapter 6 (p. 98). Follow Cauchy's argument using the partial sums of the series. Identify the sequences that increase or decrease, explain why they are bounded, and describe how Cauchy concludes that the original series has a limiting value.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Carefully read Cauchy's proof of Theorem I in Note III (p. 309). Identify the sequences Cauchy produces, explain why they are monotone and bounded, and describe how they narrow down the possible location of the desired value. At precisely what point does Cauchy conclude that his construction determines an actual real number?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Compare the two arguments. In both cases, Cauchy constructs monotone sequences that remain bounded. Why is the statement “a bounded monotone sequence of real numbers has a real limit” important to both proofs? Does Cauchy establish this property of the real numbers, or does his argument appear to assume it? Explain why establishing this property might require us to say something more about what the real numbers are.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>  

</page>
<page>

<p>
    In class, we used Dedekind's construction of the real numbers to show that
    Cauchy's two sequences of approximations determine a unique real number <m>c</m>.
    Then we have
    <md>
        x_{n}\le c\le X^{(n)}
    </md>

    and
    <md>
        X^{(n)}-x_{n}\longrightarrow 0.
    </md>
</p>

<p>
    Therefore, both the lower approximations <m>x_{n}</m> and the upper
    approximations <m>X^{(n)}</m> eventually lie arbitrarily close to <m>c</m>.
</p>

<p>
    Cauchy's construction also guarantees that the function has opposite signs
    at these approximations.
    Suppose that
</p>

<p>
    <md>
        f(x_{n})&lt;0 \qquad\text{and}\qquad f(X^{(n)})&gt;0
    </md>
</p>

<p>
    for every <m>n</m>.
</p>

<p>
    Cauchy did not state continuity using the modern
    <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>\delta</m> definition.
    For the next exercise, however, we will use that definition to make
    the final step of the argument precise.
</p>

<p>
    A function <m>f</m> is <em>continuous at <m>c</m></em> if, for every
    <m>\varepsilon&gt;0</m>, there is a <m>\delta&gt;0</m> such that
</p>

<p>
    <md>
        |x-c|&lt;\delta  \quad\Longrightarrow\quad  |f(x)-f(c)|&lt;\varepsilon.
    </md>
</p>

<exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Use this definition of continuity to show that Cauchy's <m>c</m> satisfies <m>f(c) = 0</m> as follows:
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>f(c)&gt;0</m>, then there is a <m>\delta&gt;0</m> such that
        <md>
        |x-c|&lt;\delta  \quad\Longrightarrow\quad  f(x)&gt;0.
        </md>
            </p>
            <p>
            <term>Hint:</term> Choose <m>\varepsilon=f(c)</m>, or a smaller positive number such
            as <m>\varepsilon=f(c)/2</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
        <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>f(c)&lt;0</m>, then there is a <m>\delta&gt;0</m> such that
        <md>
        |x-c|&lt;\delta  \quad\Longrightarrow\quad  f(x)&lt;0.
        </md>
            </p>
            <p>
            <term>Hint:</term> Choose <m>\varepsilon=-f(c)</m>, or a smaller positive number such
            as <m>\varepsilon=-f(c)/2</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Show that <m>f(c)</m> cannot be positive by contradiction as follows:
            </p>
            <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    If <m>f(c)&gt;0</m>, then there is some <m>\delta &gt;0</m> such that <m>|x-c|&lt;\delta</m> implies <m>f(x)&gt;0</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Explain why <m>|x_n-c|&lt; \delta</m> for some <m>n</m>. Why does this create a contradiction?
                </p>
            </li>
            </ul>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Use the same idea as the last task to show that <m>f(c)</m> also cannot be negative.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>

</exercise>



</page>
  

